@startuml
title The five incoming call types — each mapped: source, route, proof, terminal

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Segoe UI
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam ArrowColor #0b7a75
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #0b7a75
skinparam DatabaseBorderColor #6b7280
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #f3f4f6
skinparam NoteBorderColor #9ca3af
left to right direction

package "Sources" #fdfdfd {
  [Another tool] as S1
  [A third party\n(their initiative)] as S2
  [A static page\nwe own] as S3
  [Our client\n(has a session)] as S4
  [A tool we\ndispatched] as S5
}

package "Route + proof (declared in the registry)" #fbfdfd {
  [tool_to_tool\nproof: carried in the document] as R1
  [third_party_callback\nproof: api_key · vault credential\n(mTLS at the infra migration)] as R2
  [static_witness\nproof: none · onload · log only] as R3
  [client_sessioned\nproof: signed session token · per-hop nonce] as R4
  [internal_callback\nproof: our_signature · signed out, verified back] as R5
}

package "Terminal" #fdfdfd {
  [A UniversalNavigatorProxy\nor the navigator] as T1
  [The Gate\n-> the UniversalNavigator] as TG
}

S1 --> R1
S2 --> R2
S3 --> R3
S4 --> R4
S5 --> R5

R1 --> T1 : never touches the Gate
R2 --> TG
R3 --> TG
R4 --> TG
R5 --> TG

note bottom of TG
  Four types cross the Gate; only tool_to_tool
  stays internal. The Gate's check is mechanical:
  route known, proof named — verify or refuse.
  An arrival on an unmapped route is an exception.
end note
@enduml
